  "name": "Alpine butterfly",
  "description": "A fixed loop tied in the middle of a rope, which can be loaded from any direction.",
  "about": "In rescue it puts a loop partway along a rope, to clip in a rescuer, a piece of gear or another anchor without needing an end. It's also used to take a damaged section out of use: tied with the damage in its loop, the loop carries no load, and the rope either side of it takes the pull.\n\nCheck that the loop comes out of the middle of the knot, between its two wraps, and that the rope leaves the knot in a straight line on each side. Dress it so the wraps lie neatly side by side, then set it by pulling on the loop and both parts of the rope.\n\nIt holds a load on the loop, on either part of the rope, or on all three at once, and pulled from both ends with the loop slack it keeps its shape. Even after a heavy load it's usually easy to untie.",
